+// LOCAL BUILD ADAPTATION (WindowsTerminal-RTL): word order, and the two things
+// that have to agree with it.
+//
+// false (the default here) means the words stay in the order they were typed:
+// typing "سلام خوبی؟" after a prompt shows the words in that same order, left to
+// right, with the shaping and joining still done properly. Setting it true gives
+// Unicode bidi rule L2, so the first logical word ends up on the right and you
+// read the line right to left - what a browser, Word and VS Code do.
+//
+// It has to steer two decisions, and getting only one of them is worse than
+// either:
+//
+//   1. _applyBidiVisualOrder reverses whole runs of RTL clusters (rule L2).
+//   2. _mapComplex asks DirectWrite for RTL *placements*, which puts each
+//      following glyph at a negative x. The renderer accumulates advances left to
+//      right, so if the cluster order stays in typed order while the placements
+//      are RTL, every letter lands in the wrong cell.
+//
+// Turning off only (1) produced exactly that: "سلام خوبی؟" came out as
+// "؟ی‌بوخـ م‌الس", with the question mark jumping to the far left.
+static constexpr bool BidiUnicodeWordOrder = true;
